      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course! Think of quantum entanglement like two coins that are magically linked—no matter how far apart you toss them, if one lands heads-up, the other instantly lands tails-up. The trick is that before you look, neither coin has a definite side; they’re in a shared, fuzzy state. Once you check one, the other “chooses” its opposite side in the same instant, even across the universe. It’s not that they send a signal—they’re just part of one single, weird system, like two ends of the same stick. For your test, just remember: entangled particles are correlated in a way that defies everyday intuition, but they don’t transmit information faster than light. That’s the core idea.",
