      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That’s exactly the kind of question that made Einstein call it “spooky action at a distance”—he hated the idea that there might be no hidden mechanism. But here’s the thing: experiments (like Bell’s theorem tests) have shown that there *isn’t* any hidden mechanism, no secret signal, no deeper clockwork. The correlation just *is*—it’s a fundamental rule of the universe, not something being “messed with” by a hidden hand.\n\nThink of it like this: you and a friend each get a sealed envelope, but the envelopes are made of the same magical paper. When you open yours, the words on your paper instantly determine the words on your friend’s—but there’s no ink, no sender, no message. The paper itself is one single object split across two places. That’s not a mechanism; it’s just how reality behaves at that level.\n\nFor your test, just remember: no hidden variables, no faster-than-light signal, just a weird, fundamental correlation. It’s not that something is “making it happen”—it’s that the universe *is* that way, like gravity or time.",
